const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "vu:0": [
    "On the Banks Islands, Lali did not want to go to her mat. Her fibre skirt with flowers still twirled in the room. Grandmother covered the lamp with her hand, only a little, and told of Qat, the hero of those islands. 'There was a time when the day never ended, and people could not sleep.' Qat went to fetch the night so they could rest, with no fight against anyone. Lali leaned close, her eyes still wide open.",
    "In the tale, Qat asked for the night and brought it carefully. He spread it over the islands like someone unrolling a cool mat. The birds fell silent, the houses grew soft, and the body, at last, understood that it could stop. Grandmother spoke slowly, and the room darkened truly because she lowered the flame. Lali saw the window turn blue and then almost black. In the story, nobody was hurt by the day giving up its place.",
    "'I still want to play,' said Lali, and she took two steps towards the open door. Grandmother did not pull her arm or raise her voice. She opened the door just enough for them to hear the quiet sea and one insect. 'The dark that Qat brought is not a punishment, it is the care that lets tomorrow exist.' Lali stayed in the doorway, barefoot, and the fibre skirt stopped twirling.",
    "They went back inside, and Lali lay down without more argument, a flower still caught in her hair. Grandmother sat beside her until her breathing grew slow and certain. There was no fear in the dark room, only the story ending in the right place. Outside, the Banks Islands rested too, each house with its share of night. Lali thought that the next day needed that pause.",
    "In the morning, the light came back on its own. Lali got up rested, ready again for the fibre skirt and the flowers. She realised that she had liked the night, once she accepted it without argument. Grandmother was already opening the window wide. Nobody had stolen the day, it had only been kept so it could return better. If you do not want to go to bed, remember that the dark is care too. Let the night put you to rest.",
  ],
  "vu:1": [
    "On the beach at Malekula, Lali knelt on the wet sand in her fibre skirt and flowers. Her aunt put a finger in her hand. 'Here the drawings tell stories. You make one line that you never lift, and at the end the sea takes it all.' Lali began a path of curves, slowly, around a smooth shell. The line wobbled a little, but you could see it well from above. Her cousin arrived and stopped before stepping on the drawing.",
    "Aunt drew a short story beside it: a bird, a wave and a little house, all in one line. Lali copied what she could and made up the rest, a big flower in the middle of the sand. She called her cousin to look, and he crouched down without spoiling anything with his knee. For a while the sand was an open book in the sun. Lali felt proud and also in a hurry to keep it for ever.",
    "The tide came up in thin, silent tongues. The first one licked the tail of Aunt's bird and erased it without a sound. Lali put her hands in front of her own drawing, as if she could build a wall against the water. Aunt shook her head with a smile. 'It is not a loss, the story has already left the sand and gone into whoever saw it.' Lali took her hands away and let the water work.",
    "When the sand flower disappeared, Lali was silent for a moment. Then she repeated the drawing in the air, with her finger. Her cousin laughed and made the bird too, without teasing her. Aunt said that in Malekula many drawings live like this. They sit between the tide and the memory of those who saw them. Nobody cried on the beach. They gathered the fallen flowers from the skirt and left the sand smooth again.",
    "On the way, Lali told the story of the line without needing to show it drawn in her hand. The fibre skirt was speckled, and she did not shake the sand off her knees at once. She liked the idea of a beauty that you cannot keep in your pocket. Aunt held her hand as far as the firm track. If you make a drawing in the sand, show it while it exists, because the story stays in your memory.",
  ],
  "vu:2": [
    "Near Port Vila, a real little canoe waited on the sand. Lali, in her fibre skirt and flowers, wanted to push it alone. Her cousin was on the other side and pulled it towards himself too, each with a hidden idea. Aunt put her hand on the hull. She told how the canoe was born when two people joined a thought, instead of hiding it. 'Alone, the idea does not float,' she said. Lali looked at her cousin.",
    "In that story, nobody stole the boat, and there was no fight over a log. There were two people who stopped keeping the plan in a pocket and put it into the same hull. Lali and her cousin let go of their crooked pulling and both went to the stern, still apart. The canoe did not move at all, because they were pushing across each other. Aunt waited, without taking the work out of their hands.",
    "Aunt put Lali on the left and her cousin on the right. Their hands were on the same warm place on the hull. 'Now, at the same time,' she said, and counted to three. They pushed into the shallow water, with no race and no shoving between them. The canoe slid a hand's width, then another, and the sand made a soft sound. Lali felt her strength was only useful because her cousin's went the same way.",
    "They did not go out into deep water that day, and Aunt held the rope. They only followed the canoe until it floated, one on each side. Lali did not try to be the only one to get in first. She waited for her cousin to put his hand on the edge too. Then they both went back to the dry sand. The canoe was ready for whoever would really use it. The tale, for them, was already complete there.",
    "Drying her feet, Lali said that next time she would not hide her pushing idea. Her cousin said he would not pull to his own side only either, as if the canoe were his. The flowers on the fibre skirt were crooked, and nobody minded that. The canoe rocked, whole, because two people had agreed on the movement. If you have heavy work to do with someone else, do not hide it. Do not pull it for yourself alone, push at the same time.",
  ],
  "vu:3": [
    "At a village feast, Lali heard her aunt tell, among laughs, how the rat came to have a long tail. Lali, in her fibre skirt and flowers, was sitting on a mat, with a plate she wanted to fill again. In the tale, there was a full table and a small rat. He did not need to carry it all into a corner. Even so, he wanted one more piece, and another, leaving nothing for the next morning.",
    "Aunt made the rat's voice thin and hurried, and the children laughed without any unkindness. When the feast was nearly over, the rat tried to leave loaded down and got stuck in his own haste. He stretched so far to escape, without letting go of the whole table. His tail grew long behind him. Nobody hurt him or pulled his tail. Lali laughed and covered her mouth.",
    "There was still too much fruit on her plate. She was going to fetch another bunch just because it was close at hand. The story tickled her conscience, without giving her any fright. She put the bunch back in the middle and kept what she already had on her plate. Her cousin, beside her, imitated the rat running with its tail dragging, and the circle laughed with pleasure. There was no pinching and no punishment.",
    "Later, Lali saw a real rat pass far from the mat, thin and quick, and she threw nothing at it. Aunt said the tale is not for chasing animals round the yard. It is for not emptying the table while the feast is still going. Lali left a little fruit on the shared plate, for anyone who had not eaten yet. The fibre skirt brushed the ground as she stood up to make room for another child.",
    "Before sleeping, Lali was still laughing at the rat's thin voice and long tail. She realised that running away loaded down is what stretches things. She also found that a feast tastes better when something is left for tomorrow. She tidied the flowers on her skirt and closed her eyes without wanting anything more. The village was quiet, with the table already in order. If you are at a big feast, do not try to take the whole table with you.",
  ],
  "vu:4": [
    "In the cove near Port Vila, the sea was choppy. Lali, in her fibre skirt and flowers, thought it funny to whistle at the waves. Her uncle, who was coiling a rope, looked up at once. 'Here nobody whistles at a rough sea, it is an old custom and not a game.' Lali thought it was just a nice sound and whistled again, quietly, smiling at the foam. The wind carried the sound towards the water.",
    "A bigger wave climbed the sand and only wet her feet, cold and without knocking her over. Lali jumped and stopped her whistle halfway, her mouth still round. Nothing else happened, no pull and no great fear, only her dripping toes. Uncle looked at her without anger: 'The sea listens in its own way. When it is rough, you speak softly.' Lali stepped back from the foam line to where the sand was already dry.",
    "Uncle did not give her a long sermon or send her home. He showed her the difference between the cove today and on calm days, when you can sing. Lali kept her whistle behind closed lips and began to count the waves almost silently. Her fibre skirt dried in the wind, and a flower fell. She picked it up without making music for the water again. The sea grew calm slowly.",
    "When the cove was flat, Uncle let her get her feet wet again, now in agreed silence. Lali went in up to her ankle and did not whistle once. She spoke to her cousin, who came later, in an indoor voice and not a challenging one. She told him the custom and showed him her feet, already dry from the first wave. He understood and did not try the sound himself, and they both watched little boats far away.",
    "On the way back, Lali still wanted to whistle. She saved it for the earth path, away from the water. Uncle approved and even whistled with her among the trees, where the custom does not ask for silence. The flower was in her hand, and the fibre skirt no longer dripped. She had learnt the difference between playing and calling the sea at the wrong time. If you are in a rough cove, do not whistle at the waves.",
  ],
  "nc:0": [
    "On the beach at Nouméa, Téa walked in the flowery Kanak mission dress. She watched a gull opening mussels on the low rocks. The bird wanted them all, and jumped from stone to stone, leaving none for the next pool. Her grandmother, beside her, told how the gull learnt to leave the ones of the tide still to come. 'If it takes everything today, the rock is silent tomorrow.' Téa stopped with the empty basket in her hands.",
    "In Grandmother's tale, the gull filled its beak so many times that it dropped half. It still came back for more. A wave covered the pool, and the bird was left looking at the place where there was nothing now. Only then did it understand that the next tide brings what the last tide wisely keeps. Téa listened without a cruel pity for the bird. It was a lesson with feathers, told at the water's edge, as the sun dried the stones.",
    "On the rocks, Téa saw mussels shining and her hand went by itself to the basket, which could be filled. Grandmother laid her palm over it, lightly, without tapping her fingers. 'A handful, and the rest stays for the tide and for the gull that has already learnt.' Téa chose few, the ones that came loose without tearing the rock, and stopped even when the basket looked poor. The real gull, far off, landed too and did not take the whole pool.",
    "They ate a few at home, well cooked. Téa did not ask to go back the same day to fill the rest of the basket. She put the empty basket by the door, as proof that she had stopped in time. Grandmother praised the handful, not the amount that haste could have fitted in. The flowery Kanak mission dress smelt of salt and sun. Téa shook the sand off her feet and went to see the sea from the window.",
    "Before sleeping, Téa thought of the gull learning, not being punished for being hungry. The beach at Nouméa kept its mussels, and her plate had been enough for the evening. She liked that simple sum and repeated it quietly. Grandmother turned off the light early, with the window still smelling of the sea. If you pick mussels on the rock, take only a handful. Leave those of the next tide, so the beach is not silent tomorrow.",
  ],
  "nc:1": [
    "In Lifou, Téa wanted to paint until night, with the flowery Kanak mission dress already stained blue on one cuff. Grandmother lit the table lamp. She talked about the sun, the moon and a lady who let nobody keep the whole sky. 'The day works, the night rests, and the two take turns without argument.' Téa dipped her brush again, as if the story were only a background for her picture. The page was still almost white.",
    "In the tale, the sun wanted to stay, and the moon did too. The lady put each one in its turn, so people could see and then sleep. There was no quarrel that hurt anyone, there was an old rhythm made of sky. Téa painted a yellow circle and a silver one, side by side, without setting them to fight on the page. Grandmother saw and said it was just right that way. The table lamp still had a little more to give for blue.",
    "When the window went dark, Téa asked for the lamp. She wanted to finish the sea in her picture, wave by wave. Grandmother let her close one wave and then put her hand on the switch. 'The lady in the tale also turned off the lights at set hours. The sky is not hers alone until morning.' Téa sighed, washed her brush and left the page to dry away from her fingers. The flowery Kanak mission dress was hung up so it took no more paint.",
    "In bed, Téa could still see the yellow and the silver behind her eyelids. That needed no lamp switched on. Grandmother sat for a minute and did not turn the lamp back on, even when Téa asked with her eyes. Outside, Lifou had its whole night, without Téa having to paint all of it. In the morning the picture was dry and beautiful, even unfinished in one corner. She liked it better like that than if she had forced her eyes.",
    "At breakfast, Téa showed the page and did not ask to finish it before leaving the house. Grandmother said the sun was already on its turn outside, for free. Téa put on the clean flowery Kanak mission dress and stood her brush in the cup. The sky, through the window, would not fit on one page, and she accepted that with a smile. If you want to paint until night, accept that the light goes off at set hours.",
  ],
  "nc:2": [
    "In the family garden near Hienghène, several hands worked the yam earth. Téa wanted the first portion for her own plate alone. She wore the flowery Kanak mission dress tucked up so it would not drag in the wet soil. Grandmother showed her how the yam asks for more than one person. One opens the furrow, another covers, another brings water. 'Alone, the earth tires,' said Grandmother. Téa held the small hoe with both hands.",
    "Téa helped to cover one mound. Then she stood looking at the harvest basket already full, imagining the biggest piece on her plate. Her cousin passed her the hoe without letting go of it suddenly. Both of them held it at the same time. She understood that even the tool needed two hands that day. She did not pull up one yam too many to hide behind the basket. She put water where Grandmother pointed and cleaned her nails on the rim of the pail.",
    "In the kitchen, the yam was cooked in the middle of the table. Téa held out her plate first, already hungry. Grandmother did not scold her, she only waited, her own plate still empty and her cracked hands in view. Téa looked at those hands, more tired than hers, and turned the first portion to that plate. 'For you,' she said. Her cousin received the second, and she had the third, warm all the same.",
    "Nobody made a long speech about sharing. They ate, and the yam tasted of the smoke of the kitchen and the soil of the morning. Téa did not feel her plate smaller for having waited, she felt the table bigger, with room for everyone. Grandmother cut one more piece and gave it back to her, because giving also comes back to the plate. The flowery Kanak mission dress had a soil mark on the hem. Téa left it until the end of the meal.",
    "In the afternoon, Téa watered the new mound without asking for tomorrow's portion to be marked with her name. Her cousin followed with the pail, whistling quietly. The garden at Hienghène was quiet, with the work of several hands already under the earth. Téa liked not being the only owner of the first bite. If you help to plant your family's yam, do not keep the first portion for your own plate alone.",
  ],
  "nc:3": [
    "Before the big house at Hienghène, Téa looked up and saw the roof spire, tall, marking the place that welcomes. The flowery Kanak mission dress moved in the afternoon wind. Her grandfather said that spire is not a toy and not a lookout for children. 'It says that the house receives, and nobody climbs on the roof to prove it.' Téa already had one foot at the base of the wall and took it away at once, a little ashamed.",
    "A visitor was coming along the path, with a basket and a tired smile. Téa wanted to show the house by climbing a post, to look tall as well. Grandmother called her to the door, where there were flowers still to put in a vessel. 'Whoever welcomes puts beauty at the entrance, not their feet on the roof.' Téa held the flowers with both hands, choosing the freshest. The roof spire stayed in its place, quiet and enough.",
    "Together, they arranged the flowers in a wide gourd, low down. The visitor could see them without lifting her chin to the roof. Téa took off the dry leaves and did not break the good stems. When the visitor arrived, she said good morning and stepped back to leave the doorway clear. Nobody asked her for a feat up there, they asked her for a smile and a place in the shade. The flowery Kanak mission dress was part of the welcome, not of a climb.",
    "Inside, Téa brought fresh water and did not peep at the roof through a gap in the wall. The visitor praised the flowers, and she felt that praise was better than a balancing act up there. Grandfather spoke of the house as a body that opens its arms to whoever arrives. The spire, seen from the mat, seemed to point at the sky, not to offer a ladder. Téa leaned against Grandmother and let the grown-ups' talk go on without interrupting.",
    "When the visitor left, Téa helped to sweep the entrance. She did not keep the flowers for her own room alone. They stayed at the door, so the house could go on welcoming the next path. The flowery Kanak mission dress had yard dust on it, not roof dust. She shook it and looked up one last time, her feet firmly on the ground. If you see the roof spire on a welcoming house, do not climb up there.",
  ],
  "nc:4": [
    "On the sand at Lifou, Téa found a nautilus shell, curled in chambers. The flowery Kanak mission dress dragged a wet corner. The shell was empty, light, with a small hole already made by the sea. Grandmother turned it in the sun. 'This one grew slowly, chamber by chamber, and this one has no animal in it now.' Téa pressed it to her chest and at once wanted to break it open to see all inside at once.",
    "Grandmother held her fingers before she could do it, with soft firmness. 'Breaking shows no more, look in through the shell's mouth.' Téa peeped and saw the curves inside, like the rooms of a tiny house. Further on, at the water line, there was another shell with an animal still drawn in. Grandmother pointed without hurry: 'That one goes back to the sea, it is not a souvenir, it is somebody.' Téa nodded and went with her, barefoot.",
    "They returned the inhabited shell to the shallow water, without throwing it. They just set it where the wave could take it. The animal did not come out to frighten them, and they did not wait for a show. Téa washed her hands and came back to the empty shell, which Grandmother had put on a wide leaf. 'Your grandmother says this one was already empty when you found it, and that one you may take.' Téa repeated the sentence so as not to get it wrong later.",
    "Téa did not knock it against a stone to make bracelets or to hear it crack. She carried it whole in the basket, wrapped in a leaf. At home she put it on a shelf where the light showed the chambers. Her cousin wanted one the same, and she explained the difference between empty and alive. He understood and did not go back to the beach looking for animals. The flowery Kanak mission dress dried on the chair, and the shell seemed to keep growing in the story.",
    "At night, Téa ran her finger round the curves. She thought of the time one chamber takes to be made, with no human hurry. She had no wish to find another the next day. The one from Lifou was enough, because it was empty and whole. The other had gone back to the water. Grandmother turned off the light after letting her look a little longer. If you find a nautilus shell, do not break it, and give back the ones that still have an animal.",
  ],
  "pg:0": [
    "In Port Moresby, Aunt told Kora and her cousin the story of Kilibob and Manup. They were two brothers who stopped arguing to swap pots and canoe voyages. Kora wore the bilas with plumes of paradise birds and listened cross-legged on the mat. In the tale there was no battle and no shouting. There was a sea path and objects passing from hand to hand. Her cousin played with a bracelet, without looking at Aunt.",
    "Kilibob knew some things, Manup knew others. While each kept his knowledge to himself, the canoe did not leave the shore. When they agreed to swap, the pots went one way and the voyages the other. Both shores grew fuller without anyone losing their voice. Kora liked that part and sat up straight on the mat. Aunt put one of Kora's drawings in one hand and her cousin's bracelet in the other. 'See if you are still angry about a colour.'",
    "That morning they had argued because both of them wanted the same sheet of paper and the same seed bracelet. Now, on the mat, Kora held out the drawing without pulling it back. Her cousin held out the bracelet and did not close her hand round it at the last moment either. Kora fastened the bracelet on her wrist, over the bilas, and her cousin held the drawing like a map. Aunt did not choose a winner, she just filled two cups with water.",
    "They went out to the shade of the yard, no longer with closed faces. They agreed who would keep the drawing for two days and who would keep the bracelet. It was a swap with time in it, like the canoe voyages in the tale. It was not a trick by one of them. Kora did not hide a second drawing behind her back. Her cousin did not ask for the bracelet back before the agreed time. The bilas with paradise plumes moved when they jumped rope together.",
    "At night, Kora still had the bracelet and did not feel she had lost the drawing for ever. Her cousin came to show her a detail she had added on the sheet, a little boat between two waves. The two of them laughed, close together on the mat. Kilibob and Manup, in the story, had also ended up at peace on the other side of the argument. If you argue with someone over wanting the same thing, swap instead of letting it run on.",
  ],
  "pg:1": [
    "That night, still in Port Moresby, Kora wanted the torch on until morning. The bilas with plumes of paradise birds rested on a chair. Aunt told how the sun grew jealous of the moon. People looked at the moon too, and it also shone in the sky. 'The lesson was not to put anyone out, it was for each one to keep its own time.' Kora held the torch against her chest, as if the light were a secret only for her. Her cousin was already yawning on the mat.",
    "In the tale, the sun and the moon learnt to take turns. Nobody left the story hurt or without brightness. The day stayed clear for work, the night stayed dark for sleep, and the two stopped wanting the same hour. Kora heard it and still switched the torch on again when Aunt turned it off. Her cousin, on the next mat, covered her eyes with her arm. 'I can't sleep like this,' she murmured, almost voiceless.",
    "Kora saw her cousin's arm and felt a small jealousy. It was the feeling of someone who wants the light because it looks lovely in her hand. She switched the torch off and set it on the floor, away from the mat. The room had the blue window, enough not to be a hole. Aunt neither clapped nor scolded, she only said that the sun had to learn to leave as well. Kora put her hands under the blanket, so they would not look for the button alone in the dark.",
    "Her cousin thanked her in a sleepy voice and turned to the wall, calmer already. Kora lay watching a little of the sky, where there was no sun or angry moon, only a calm shine. The bilas rested on the chair, and the plumes did not ask for an audience at that hour. When her eyes grew heavy, she did not force them open with light. The whole house seemed to breathe better, and nobody was sad that the torch was quiet.",
    "In the morning the light came back through the window, big and free. Kora did not need the torch to find her sandals. Her cousin was in good spirits, which was worth more than one clear night for only one of them. Aunt opened the door and let the day come into its turn. Kora hung up the bilas with plumes of paradise birds and went out to the yard. If you want a torch all night, turn it off so others can sleep.",
  ],
  "pg:2": [
    "On a forest path near Port Moresby, Kora saw a bird of paradise perched on a high branch. Her bilas already carried paradise plumes, gathered another way, without chasing. The bird opened its bright chest in the light and did not come down. On a leaf a long feather lay fallen, clean, with nobody pulling it. Kora crouched, and her cousin whispered that this one was enough. Kora nodded, but her eyes went to the bird.",
    "She took a step towards the trunk. She thought of climbing just a little and asking by hand for one more plume. The bird hopped to a higher branch, without panic, just moving away calmly. Kora stopped halfway through the movement. She remembered that a hair plume must be found, not plucked from a living body. Her cousin held the band of the bilas, lightly, like someone recalling an agreement. 'If you run after it, you lose the one already on the ground.'",
    "Kora stepped back, picked up the fallen feather and did not shake the branch to make more fall. The bird watched, still, and then went on its way among the high leaves. Nobody touched its wings or asked for more brightness from it. The feather in Kora's hand was long enough for her hair, and it shone without needing company. She fixed it carefully, beside the others on the bilas, and felt the ornament was right like that.",
    "On the way back they did not speak loudly. The forest could keep the rest of its birds at peace. Kora showed the feather to Aunt, who asked straight away whether it had come from the ground. 'It fell, I did not take it from a body,' said Kora, and Aunt smiled without needing a second question. Her cousin drew the bird in a corner of the sheet, from memory, which took no feathers from it either. The road home was easy.",
    "At home, the new plume moved when Kora walked. She did not miss the ones that had stayed on the bird. The bilas with paradise plumes was complete without a hunt among the leaves. Aunt poured water for the two of them. She praised the shine that was found, not the kind that is pulled. Kora looked through the window at the closed forest and left it closed. If you see a bird of paradise, do not run after it for more feathers.",
  ],
  "pg:3": [
    "On the bay at Milne, Kora sat beside a woman making pots. Her bilas with paradise plumes was kept away, so it would not catch clay. The woman said that people there learnt to make pots with patience, coil by coil. Nobody presses haste against a wall still soft. Kora asked to help and was given a lump of clean clay. They showed her the open palm. 'Smooth it, do not squash it,' they said, and she nodded seriously.",
    "Kora began well, in slow circles. Then she wanted to see a tall pot at once, like the ones on the shelf in the shade. She pressed too hard, the side gave way and left an ugly dent. Nobody laughed at her unkindly. The woman wet her fingers, raised the wall again and gave her the clay once more, smaller. 'Haste makes a hole, and a calm hand makes a house for water.' Kora took a deep breath and began from the start.",
    "This time she smoothed until the rim was even. She stopped when they told her to stop, without the last squeeze of vanity. She did not mark the clay with her nail to show it had been her. The little pot dried in the shade, beside bigger ones that had taken days. Kora washed her hands and did not touch the still soft piece to show her cousin. Her cousin came, saw it from a distance and praised the shape without picking it up.",
    "While they waited, the woman told them that a hurried pot cracks in the fire and loses everyone's food. Kora looked at her own, modest and whole, and liked it more than a tall crooked one. She helped to cover the leftover clay with a damp leaf, so it would still be useful the next day. She did not throw the rest away out of impatience. The bay outside was shining, and the work inside asked for the same slowness as the tide.",
    "When the pot was dry enough to carry away wrapped up, Kora walked slowly home, without running. She did not press it against the bilas with paradise plumes, afraid of denting it again. She put it on the table and waited for Aunt to see it without putting it straight on the fire. Aunt ran her hand over the rim and smiled. If you help to make a clay pot, smooth it with patience and do not squash it in a hurry.",
  ],
  "pg:4": [
    "On the bend of the Sepik river, Kora stopped because Aunt stopped. Both of them carried the bilas with paradise plumes without dragging it in the branches. 'Here lives the masalai, the owner of the place, and you greet it,' said Aunt. 'It is not a figure to frighten, but the spirit of the place.' Kora saw nobody among the trees. She saw dark water turning and thick bush on the inner side of the bend. Aunt bowed her head with respect.",
    "Kora did the same, in a low voice, and said good morning to the owner of the bend. She promised that they would keep to the open path. She did not go into the bush just to prove her courage to her cousin, who was coming behind. Aunt said that respect, there, means not pushing your body where the place has not called you. Kora looked at the green tunnel and left it green. Her feet went to the clear bank, where others had walked.",
    "A younger cousin wanted to take a short cut through it, laughing. Kora held his hand before the first branch. 'Greet it and stay on the path,' she said, repeating Aunt without adding any scares. He greeted it too, a little embarrassed, and fell back behind her. The river went on turning without blocking their way. No shape came out of the trees, and the place stayed calm. The open path went on easily under their feet.",
    "Further on, the path opened into a clearing where they could drink and rest in the shade. Kora did not look for a short cut back through the thick bush. It looked shorter in her head, but she did not. They followed the bend on the outside, as they had told the owner of the place. The bilas with paradise plumes did not catch on thorns. Aunt gave her water and praised the sentence spoken at the entrance.",
    "At home, Kora said she had greeted a bend in the river. The thick bush, she said, had been left unentered. Nobody added teeth, eyes or chases in the dark. The story of the masalai stayed light, about respect and the right path. She hung up the bilas and washed the river dust off her feet. If you pass a place that has an owner, greet it and do not go into the thick bush. The easy way is the one made with respect.",
  ],
  "tf:0": [
    "On the cliff of Amsterdam Island, Polo went in a polar expedition coat and hat. He followed his mother and the guide. An albatross passed so close that its shadow covered the path. The bird opened its huge wings and made a wide circle. Then it headed back towards a nest down below, on a ledge. Polo felt his legs wanting to run after it. His mother pointed to the safety rope. 'Up to here, and not one step to the side.'",
    "The guide said quietly that the albatross makes an immense journey and returns to the same nest. It needs no child to follow it on the edge. Polo put his hand on the rope and did not let go, even when the wind pushed him. Down below, the bird settled beside another, the two still against the cold. There was no hurry in the nest, there was a return. Polo understood that running on the cliff was not part of that story.",
    "Even so, when the albatross rose again, Polo took two steps to the side, parallel to the rope. It was as if he could follow it on the ground. The guide stood in between without shouting in his ear. 'Its circle is in the air, and yours is behind the rope.' Polo stopped and followed the bird only with his eyes, until it was a line in the grey. The polar expedition coat flapped in the wind, he gripped the collar, and nobody went near the edge.",
    "In the hut that night, Polo drew the albatross's circle on paper. It was a line that left and came back to the same point. His mother said that was the true adventure: setting off far and finding the nest again. He did not ask to go back to the cliff in the dark, with his hat already in his hand. He kept the drawing inside his coat, and the guide praised his quiet feet. Outside the wind went on, and the birds slept on the ledge.",
    "Polo fell asleep thinking of the huge shadow and the small nest, both fitting into the same story. He liked knowing that the bird returns, and liked even more having stayed whole to tell it in the morning. His hat lay at the foot of the bed, and the rope, in memory, had been a friend. The coat dried on a hook. If you see an albatross on a cliff, do not run after it on the edge.",
  ],
  "tf:1": [
    "On Crozet Island, Polo raised the binoculars with his hands tucked into the sleeves of his polar expedition coat. His hat almost covered one eye. Down below, the king penguins stood together. One of them had an egg resting on its feet, covered by a fold of skin. His mother whispered that you do not go down to the colony out of curiosity. 'The egg needs warmth and quiet, not a visit.' Polo stayed at the viewpoint marked with little flags.",
    "He wanted to go down only as far as the first stone. He wanted to see the shell close up and count the spots. The guide shook his head and showed him the line once more, put there on purpose. 'From here, with the binoculars, you see the egg and do not chill it with your shadow.' Polo put the binoculars to his eyes. He saw the penguin shift its weight with the care of someone who is not playing. There was no hurry in that slow dance.",
    "A cold wind came in through his collar. Polo understood a little of what it would be like to take warmth from an egg. He pulled the polar expedition coat tighter and did not insist on the forbidden path. His mother passed him his gloves, which he had taken off to focus the picture better. With warm hands, the binoculars stopped wobbling. He saw two adults so close together they looked one shadow with feet. Nobody descended.",
    "In the hut, Polo told what he had seen without making the egg bigger to impress anyone. He drew a penguin with covered feet and showed his mother, who recognised the scene. The guide said that watching like this is learning too. The line of flags is also a form of care. Polo hung up his hat and did not ask to go into the colony the next day. The hot soup arrived, and he thought of the bird's feet, warm in their own way.",
    "Before sleeping, Polo looked through the little window and saw only the dark. He was glad the colony was far away. The polar expedition coat dried on a hook, with salt on the sleeve. He had the binoculars in their case, closed, waiting for another viewpoint. The story of the egg on the feet stayed whole because he had not gone to touch it. If you see a king penguin with an egg on its feet, do not go near the colony.",
  ],
  "tf:2": [
    "In the Kerguelen hut, the cook put the soup on the table. Polo, in his polar expedition coat and hat in his hand, asked about the island's famous cabbage. She shook her head with sympathy and pushed his already full bowl towards him. 'Kerguelen cabbage saved meals long ago, and the sailors learnt not to pull it all up.' Polo looked at the steaming pot, and then at the door, still shut against the wind.",
    "The cook told how, at first, some people took the rare plant. They thought it would grow again the next day. Then people understood that a ship's hunger cannot finish off the greens of a whole island. The lesson stayed in the hut, repeated at every meal: you eat what has already been prepared by rule. Polo pushed his chair back, halfway through a plan, and sat down again. His spoon was beside the plate, waiting for him.",
    "He still asked whether one leaf only, from a place where there were many, would do no harm at all. The cook showed him a slope through the window, where the plants were few and low. It was nothing like an endless garden. 'Today you pick nothing, not one leaf, because the soup is made and the cabbage out there is still growing.' Polo nodded slowly. He did not put on his boots or open the hut door with a bag in his hand.",
    "He ate all the soup, hot. He asked only for another piece of bread, not for a walk to the rare plants. His mother praised the question and praised the door that stayed shut even more. The hat was drying near the stove, smelling of wet wool. Outside the wind was strong, and the Kerguelen cabbage stayed in its place, with no small hand testing it. Polo washed his bowl, which is the work of someone who has eaten.",
    "At night, in his bunk, Polo told his mother the story of the sailors again. Then he added the important part: he had picked nothing. His mother said that was the new page of the tale, his own. The polar expedition coat hung on the hook, and the boots were lined up. There was no cabbage soil in the tread. The soup still warmed his stomach. If you find a rare plant that once saved meals, do not pull it up.",
  ],
  "tf:3": [
    "On the big beach at Kerguelen, an elephant seal slept like a soft boulder. Polo stopped in his polar expedition coat and hat. He wanted a close photograph, to see the whiskers and the wrinkle of the snout. The guide raised his hand at the distance marked by two low stakes. 'This animal needs the beach quiet, and your camera stays here.' Polo had the camera on his chest and his feet already leaning forward.",
    "His mother crouched beside him. His eyes were at the height of the stake, not at the height of hurry. 'If he wakes because of us, the beach stops being a bed.' Polo took half a step back, to the exact place the guide had shown with his boot. He raised the camera without taking one more step, only from where he stood. The elephant seal did not move a flipper. Its sleep was heavy and deserved.",
    "Polo took one image only, from far off. He did not ask to crawl one more metre on the cold sand. The guide looked and nodded: you could make out the animal, and that was enough for memory. Someone else from the expedition passed behind them, silently too, without coming near the stakes. Polo's coat made a fabric noise in the wind, and he stood still so as not to repeat it. Nobody called the animal by its size or its name.",
    "When they went back along the staked path, the elephant seal was still sleeping, exactly as they had found it. Polo put the camera in the bag and did not mind the marked distance. His mother said that a good photograph, there, is one that wakes nobody on the beach. His hat slipped, and he caught it without running. The beach stayed quiet behind them, with its enormous guest at peace.",
    "In the hut, Polo showed the image to the cook, small and honest. She liked the account more than the detail that was missing. He did not make up a close encounter to look brave. What he had done was respect, and that could be seen in his calm face. The polar expedition coat went on the hook, with sand in the hem. If you want a photograph of an elephant seal, stop at the distance the guide marks.",
  ],
  "tf:4": [
    "That night, in the Kerguelen hut, someone called quietly at the door. Polo went out in his hat and polar expedition coat to see the sky. A green and pink light moved slowly, like a blanket being pulled over the stars. His mother put her arm round his shoulders: 'It is the southern lights, not an angry spirit. It is a blanket of light.' They could watch it for a while, and Polo forgot the sleep in his eyes.",
    "He wanted to stay awake all night, until the last fold of colour faded on the horizon. He sat on a crate, chin up, and counted the changes: green, then a paler ribbon, then almost blue. His mother did not spoil his wonder with a hurry to go in. She gave him that time, as she had promised, and stayed beside him without turning the sky into a fear. The wind was cold, the coat did its work, and the light asked for nothing in return.",
    "When the agreed time was up, Polo asked for more, and a little more, without getting up from the crate. His mother pointed to the bed, visible through the open door of the hut. 'The blanket of the sky stays, even if you go to sleep. Tomorrow your eyes will need all of you.' He looked one last time, slowly, to keep the shape of the ribbons. Then he went in and took off his hat.",
    "Already lying down, Polo left a gap at the window, as agreed. A bit of glow could come in without keeping him on his feet. He did not go out again when he saw a stronger flash. He breathed deeply and let the wool blanket do its part, while the sky's blanket did its own outside. His mother stayed until his voice grew slower. In the hut the others slept too, and the lights went on, generously, over the roof.",
    "In the morning, Polo told about the light as someone telling a true dream. He gave it no angry face and no danger. His mother served his breakfast and said the sky would still be there another night, if the weather allowed. The polar expedition coat waited on the hook, ready for the cold, not for an endless vigil. He had seen enough and was rested. If you see the southern lights like a blanket in the sky, watch the time you are given.",
  ],
  "bs:0": [
    "In Nassau, the night was still dark when Maya's family went into the street. They carried small bells and low drums. Maya wore the crepe paper costume, light and colourful, and a flower over her ear. The music was already calling the morning, even with not a drop of sun in the sky. Her mother held her hand calmly and walked beside her. 'We stay with the group, with no shoving,' she said.",
    "The procession moved slowly between the houses, and the crepe paper brushed Maya's arms in time with the bells. She wanted to run ahead to be the first to see the square lit up. Her cousin, beside her, wore a fringed suit as fine as hers. When Maya sped up, her shoulder nearly tore one of his fringes. She stopped at once and held her pace.",
    "'Sorry,' said Maya, and straightened the fringe with two fingers, without pulling or laughing. Her cousin smiled and was not cross about the near bump. Their aunt, further back, nodded, because the group had stayed whole. Nobody pushed anybody to be seen further up the line. The bells went back to the same rhythm, as if the street were one single person.",
    "They went on like that until the sky turned grey and then peach colour over the roofs. Maya no longer wanted to leave the line: she liked hearing her mother's bell right beside her own. When a small boy stumbled, the group made space and waited, with no laughter and no hurry. Maya's crepe paper stayed whole, and so did her cousin's suit. The morning arrived slowly, called by music and not by shouting.",
    "In the square, now with light, the family stopped and drank water, still together and not spreading out. Maya ran her hand over her paper costume and saw that she had not spoilt anyone else's. Her cousin thanked her again, quietly, and she felt part of the group, not ahead of it. The drums fell silent without haste. If you join a festival like this, stay with your own people, and treat the other costume as your own.",
  ],
  "bs:1": [
    "On Andros Island, Maya walked with her uncle along a path of tall pines. She still wore the crepe paper costume and the flower in her hair. The ground was soft with dry needles, and the air smelt of warm resin. They heard a small laugh high up, among the branches, and it was not from any child in the family. Her uncle spoke quietly, so as not to scare the wood. 'There is a tiny tree being here, and it likes good manners.'",
    "Maya looked up and saw a figure the size of a shoe, with shining eyes and a quiet smile. It seemed so small that a laugh came out of her, without unkindness, but too loud for that place. 'What a tiny little thing,' she said, pointing with her finger. The little being hid behind a wide leaf. Her uncle sighed, without shouting, and asked her to go on carefully.",
    "The path, which had gone straight on before, began to come back to the same tree. It had a mark of resin. Maya counted the turns: one, two, three, and her uncle's basket appeared again in the same place. There was no fog and no fright, only a stubborn path that would not open the door. Her uncle stopped and looked at her calmly. 'When you mock someone, the forest asks for an apology,' he said.",
    "Maya put her palm on the trunk and spoke to the branches, in a clear voice and with no laughter. 'I am sorry, you are small,' she said, 'and I should not have pointed at you.' She waited with the flower trembling a little in her hair. A twig moved like a nod, and the laugh came back, friendly this time. The ground ahead showed light footprints that did not go round. Her uncle held her shoulder.",
    "They came out of the pine wood into a clearing with light, without losing the basket or each other. The tiny being stayed in the tree, quiet and content. Maya did not laugh at it again or point at the branches. She pressed the flower in her hair and thanked it in a low voice. The path was already open behind her. If you meet someone smaller than you, do not mock them. You can say sorry, and the path opens again.",
  ],
  "bs:2": [
    "Maya's uncle took her out in a shallow boat near the reef. Her crepe paper costume was firmly fastened, so the wind would not take it. The water by the hull was clear, and you could see small fish like crumbs of light. Further on, the blue turned dark all at once, like a door shut on the sea floor. Her uncle pointed with his chin, without standing up. 'The fishermen call that deep water Lusca, and nobody dives there alone,' he said.",
    "Maya leaned over, curious, just to peep at the edge where the clear water ends and the dark begins. The flower in her hair hung down towards the water and almost touched it. She wanted to see a little more, just a hand's width, and her arm was already ahead of her knee. No shapes came out of the sea, no arm, no mouth. There was only depth, quiet and great, which was no place to play.",
    "Her uncle put a firm hand on her waist and drew her to the middle of the shallow boat. He did not frighten her with shouts. 'The eyes are enough,' he said. 'The deep is kept for those who know how to come back, and you are still learning to sit down.' Maya sat on the dry bench and held the edge with both hands. The boat did not rock much, and the crepe paper stayed away from the water.",
    "They watched the reef from the side, where the water is gentle. They did not get out of the boat that day. Her uncle told her that Lusca is not an invitation. It is the name of the place where you wait and turn back. A silver fish passed through the clear water and went on into the dark, without asking for company. Maya waved her fingers at it and did not jump. Her uncle praised the bench she had chosen.",
    "When they got back to the sand, Maya was still looking at the blue line. Her feet were on the warm ground, and her uncle said he had liked seeing her stay in the boat. She saved her questions for the shore, not for a dive, because there was nothing to beat down there. The crepe paper was dry, and the flower was still in place. If you see the water turning dark, stay in the shallow boat with someone who knows.",
  ],
  "bs:3": [
    "In the straw market at Nassau, Maya's grandmother sat on a low bench, with golden strips on her lap. Maya arrived in her crepe paper costume and flower. She sat right beside her, in a hurry to have a basket. She wanted to take shells home that very afternoon. Her grandmother showed her one single coil, slowly, like someone telling a story with her fingers. 'First the coil, then the basket,' she said.",
    "Maya tried to copy it, and the strip escaped her, soft, with no shape of a basket at all. She huffed, crossed her arms and looked at the ready-made baskets on the stall in front. 'This takes too long,' she said, stamping her foot on the earthen floor. Her grandmother did not laugh or take the straw from her hands. She laid her hand over Maya's and started the same coil again, more slowly still.",
    "On the third try, the coil came out round and held itself, like a firm ring. Maya smiled, surprised, and wanted to make five the same at once, to finish the base. Her grandmother shook her head gently and held her fidgeting fingers. 'One coil well made is worth more than ten in a hurry,' she said. Maya breathed and repeated only that one, until her hands found the way without help.",
    "When the coil settled, her grandmother let her add another, and another, always with time to spare. They did not finish the basket that day. Maya was not cross and did not throw the straw on the floor. She kept the circle in a clean cloth, like a small treasure that was still going to grow. Her grandmother sold a big basket to a lady and thanked her without hurry. Maya helped tidy the strips in the stall basket.",
    "On the way out, Maya carried her coil of straw on her arm, not a basket bought in a hurry. She understood that her grandmother's hands knew how to wait. She promised to come back the next day for the next coil, without huffing or crossing her arms. The flower in her hair was crooked from the wind, and she laughed at that, not at her grandmother. If you learn something with your hands, do one coil at a time.",
  ],
  "bs:4": [
    "On the harbour island, Maya's uncle showed her a large conch, sand-coloured outside and pink inside. She was in her crepe paper costume, with the flower held by a cloth pin. The coast wind was lively. Her cousin had gone in a boat to a further jetty, still in sight, small as a finger. Her uncle held the shell with both hands, without blowing it. 'This calls those out at sea, it is not for frightening anyone,' he said.",
    "Maya wanted to blow it at once, just to hear the big sound come out of the pink shell. She put the conch to her mouth, and her uncle laid a finger on her wrist, asking her to wait. 'Only when I say, and only once, so your cousin knows we can see him,' he explained calmly. Maya lowered the shell and watched the boat in the clear water. There was no storm and no shout, only a message to agree.",
    "Her uncle made a wide wave with his arm, and her cousin in the boat waved back, slowly. Then he said the agreed word, short and clear, and Maya blew calmly, once only. A round sound came out, like a friendly signal, which hurt nobody's ears and was not repeated for fun. Her cousin raised his hat, showing he had heard from far off. Her uncle smiled and took the shell from her carefully, to set it on the dry sand.",
    "While the boat came towards the jetty, Maya asked if she could blow it for the birds, just for music. Her uncle shook his head and put the conch in the cloth. He explained that, if they blew it for no reason, nobody would know when the message was real. She understood and kept her hands in her lap, watching her cousin row the last stretch. The crepe paper crackled in the wind. Her cousin arrived.",
    "The three of them sat in the shade of a porch and drank cool water. The conch lay on a clean cloth. Maya did not blow it again, even with the urge tickling her mouth. She liked having waited for her uncle's word. The shell stayed pink and quiet, ready for another day when the message would be real. If you have a voice that carries far, use it when you are asked, not to frighten. You can call someone without scaring them.",
  ],
};
export default PART;
